    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You propose a fascinating Bayesian structure, but structuring the likelihood function to account for time-lags while treating the spike as a prior introduces significant challenges related to causality and autocorrelation. In a high-frequency setting, the time-lag means the observed market data is no longer the immediate target of the spike, but a potential confirmation of an event that may have already been priced in, rendering the \"prediction\" retrospective. Furthermore, without an external validation dataset showing that these spikes occur more frequently than Poisson processes allow during the 30-60 second window, the model risks overfitting to your own recall bias. If you proceed with this framework, you would need to rigorously define a null hypothesis that no such predictive power exists, as standard financial theory currently attributes such timing capabilities to information asymmetry or Algorithms rather than subjective internal signals.",
